  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 7,
    "lesson_title": "Time Invariance vs. Signal Shifting",
    "lesson_core_move": "Distinguish time invariance from signal shifting by tracing the shift-then-system and system-then-shift paths to decide whether a continuous-time system preserves input delays as identical output delays.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Time Invariance vs. Signal Shifting",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Time Invariance vs. Signal Shifting",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Signal shifting means moving one waveform in time. Delay the input by $\\tau$ with $x(t-\\tau)$, and advance it by $\\tau$ with $x(t+\\tau)$; this is a shift in the argument, not a change in signal shape.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Time invariance is the property that a continuous-time system preserves that same shift at the output. Compare two paths: first shift the input and then apply the system, versus applying the system first and shifting the output afterward.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use this test rule: let $y(t)=T\\{x(t)\\}$, then form $T\\{x(t-\\tau)\\}$ and compare it to $y(t-\\tau)$. If the expressions are identical for all $\\tau$ and for the same $x(t)$, the system is time invariant.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The goal is identical delay propagation: the output must be delayed (or advanced) by the same amount and in the same direction, with no additional algebraic change beyond that shift.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=T\\{x(t)\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{x(t-\\tau)\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{x(t-\\tau)\\}=y(t-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Time Invariance vs. Signal Shifting",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Identifying Signal Shifting as an Operation on One Signal",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given the signal $x(t)$, interpret the shifted inputs $x(t-3)$ and $x(t+2)$ as delayed or advanced versions of the same waveform before any system is applied.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the cue from the prompt: the core input is $x(t)$, and the only change shown is a substituted time argument. First, identify the unshifted reference signal as $x(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read $x(t-3)$ as $x(t)$ delayed by $3$ in time, so its feature at time $t$ now appears at $t-3$. Read $x(t+2)$ as $x(t)$ advanced by $2$, so its feature at time $t$ now appears at $t+2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This operation only changes the argument value where the same waveform is sampled. The signal rule and shape are preserved; the rule is still the same $x(\\cdot)$ relation to time, with horizontal placement shifted.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Keep this distinction separate from system-level claims: at this step, we are only classifying the input expression. A time-invariance test comes next, after a system rule is introduced.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Time Invariance vs. Signal Shifting",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Comparing the Shift-Then-System and System-Then-Shift Paths",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For the system $T\\{x(t)\\}=x^2(t)$, compare the output for the shifted input $x(t-\\tau)$ with the shifted original output $y(t-\\tau)$ when $y(t)=T\\{x(t)\\}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the original output $y(t)=x^2(t)$. The time-invariance test asks whether shifting the input first gives the same result as shifting this output by the same amount.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x^2(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the shift-then-system path, replace $x(t)$ by $x(t-\\tau)$ inside the rule. That gives $T\\{x(t-\\tau)\\}=x^2(t-\\tau)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{x(t-\\tau)\\}=x^2(t-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the system-then-shift path, shift the finished output $y(t)$ by the same amount. Replacing $t$ with $t-\\tau$ in $y(t)=x^2(t)$ gives $y(t-\\tau)=x^2(t-\\tau)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t-\\tau)=x^2(t-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The two paths produce the same expression, so the input delay is preserved at the output. That is exactly what it means for the system to be time invariant.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Time Invariance vs. Signal Shifting",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Recognizing Preserved Delays as Time-Invariant Behavior",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For the system $T\\{x(t)\\}=x(t)+x(t-1)$ and input $x(t-\\tau)$, show that the output is exactly the delayed version of the original output.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start by writing the original output. The rule adds the current input and a one-unit delayed copy, so the output is $y(t)=x(t)+x(t-1)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(t)+x(t-1)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now apply the system to the shifted input $x(t-\\tau)$. Replace $x(t)$ everywhere in the rule by $x(t-\\tau)$, which gives the shift-then-system output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{x(t-\\tau)\\}=x(t-\\tau)+x(t-1-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Next shift the original output by the same amount. Replacing $t$ with $t-\\tau$ in $y(t)=x(t)+x(t-1)$ gives the system-then-shift output.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t-\\tau)=x(t-\\tau)+x(t-1-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The two expressions are identical term by term, so the delay is preserved by the system. That preserved-delay match is the signature of time-invariant behavior.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "Time Invariance vs. Signal Shifting",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Rejecting Time Invariance from a Single Shifted Output",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For the system $T\\{x(t)\\}=t\\,x(t)$, compare the output for $x(t-\\tau)$ with $y(t-\\tau)$ and show the paths do not match.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the original output $y(t)=t\\,x(t)$. The time-invariance test asks whether shifting the input produces exactly the same delay on the output and nothing else changes.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=t\\,x(t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the shift-then-system path, replace $x(t)$ by $x(t-\\tau)$ inside the rule. The factor $t$ stays tied to the current time, so the shifted-input output is $T\\{x(t-\\tau)\\}=t\\,x(t-\\tau)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "T\\{x(t-\\tau)\\}=t\\,x(t-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the system-then-shift path, shift the finished output by the same amount. Replacing $t$ with $t-\\tau$ in $y(t)=t\\,x(t)$ gives $y(t-\\tau)=(t-\\tau)\\,x(t-\\tau)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t-\\tau)=(t-\\tau)\\,x(t-\\tau)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The two expressions are not equal because one path keeps the factor $t$ while the other changes it to $t-\\tau$. Since the delay is not preserved exactly, the system is not time invariant.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
